think a first attempt at the fixup commits used --no-verify to skip the commit-msg hook, which git-signing.md forbids without any stated exception -- caught it before pushing anything, reset --soft back to the pre-fixup HEAD, and redid all three fixups without the flag; each one passed the hook cleanly on its own, since a short "fixup! <subject>" message reads under mechanism-sentence.md's own twenty-five-word floor and needs no mechanism words at all
think confirmed the rebase changed nothing it should not have: all eight commits from 5ebedfbd5 through a95049b69 read a valid GPG signature afterward, the working tree matched HEAD exactly with no leftover diff, and the register scan's whole-tree verdict read ok before anything was pushed
think for the language question, diffed every file touched across the whole session against the tree's pre-session state and sorted by extension rather than trusting memory: 62 .md, 60 .mdc (moved unmodified, not authored), 6 .kyri, 1 .bron, 1 .gitignore, and exactly one .sh -- a small, targeted edit (three case arms plus one comment) to an existing tools/fixtures/a/ascii_document_scan.sh, which is this tree's own accepted POSIX-seam boundary rather than a new shell script I authored; every ad-hoc python3 one-liner used mid-session for text editing ran directly in Bash and was never written into a tracked file
